# Hardware video decode for the kiosk browser.
#
# UniTotem shows images, video and web pages, and all three go through
# Chromium - the manager drives it over the DevTools protocol rather than
# embedding a renderer of its own. So the codecs and the acceleration that
# matter are Chromium's, not GStreamer's, whatever else the image ships.
#
# Chromium leaves video decode acceleration OFF by default on Linux. The
# image already enabled --enable-gpu-rasterization (Debian's own
# default-flags), which covers compositing but not video: without the flags
# below a 1080p H.264 stream is decoded on the CPU, on a board that has a
# dedicated decoder sitting idle.
#
# The feature names here were checked against the actual binary rather than
# taken from documentation, because they change between Chromium releases and
# a misspelled one is silently ignored - which looks exactly like acceleration
# being unavailable. Confirmed present in 150.0.7871.181:
# AcceleratedVideoDecodeLinuxGL, AcceleratedVideoDecodeLinuxZeroCopyGL,
# VaapiVideoDecoder. Not present, and therefore deliberately not requested:
# VaapiVideoDecodeLinuxGL, V4L2FlatVideoDecoder, PlatformHEVCDecoderSupport.
#
# That last absence is worth knowing: HEVC will not play in this browser at
# all, confirmed by asking it (MediaSource.isTypeSupported says no), even
# though a Pi 4 has an HEVC decoder at /dev/video19. H.264, AAC, MP3, VP9,
# AV1 and Opus all report supported.
#
# --ignore-gpu-blocklist: Chromium blocklists a great many Linux GPU/driver
# combinations by default and falls back to software. On an appliance whose
# whole job is playing video on hardware someone chose, that default is the
# wrong trade.

export CHROMIUM_FLAGS="$CHROMIUM_FLAGS --ignore-gpu-blocklist"
export CHROMIUM_FLAGS="$CHROMIUM_FLAGS --enable-features=@UNITOTEM_ACCEL_FEATURES@"
